{"id":"6c44a4e515c5bedfc69024b12726b6dc51124d3749ab38f634ffdb4caa00ccbf","at":"2026-10-06T19:47:54Z","commit":"07848d66536b1a3bad4d89be4a23736a2193e195","parents":["9e306ce4240f38d137ef0e7d6f80211a8fb27582d1085e960039f9467e3688df"],"op":"relate","type":"verifies","ends":[{"kind":"test","id":"Depdep.Provider.GitTest: enumerate/1: one unit per repository, keyed on the epoch","hash":"991b6c7a"},{"kind":"spec","id":"spec/05-units-and-providers.md#git","hash":"401cf59b"}],"by":"Doug ClymerOlson <doug@yarina.org>","note":"Reviewed the test against the section after #154 reworded one sentence of it. The section's normative content is the key: objects keyed on the repository and a monthly epoch rather than a commit, with epoch/0 naming the epoch, slug/1 the repository and mirror_path/1 where a mirror lands. The test asserts enumerate/1 produces exactly one unit per repository whose object path carries the slug and the current epoch, which exercises all three. The reworded sentence states the RATIONALE for that cheap key — a stale mirror is not a wrong answer — and changes no behaviour: it replaced the near-tautological 'a wrong mix object is a build that compiles clean, passes its tests and is wrong' with 'a wrong object still compiles clean and still passes its tests'. Same claim, said once.","basis":"review"}
