{"id":"0ee7c6e243018eac7fe26daf49e12038b18ee32c469539188abf95ebd50216b8","at":"2026-10-06T19:49:50Z","commit":"07848d66536b1a3bad4d89be4a23736a2193e195","parents":["3f302f102ad3e7fc82f1488d99b7eb071d1f1ebbd2eb003f908d661df596f76a"],"op":"relate","type":"verifies","ends":[{"kind":"test","id":"Depdep.Provider.GitTest: slug/1: https and ssh forms of one repository agree","hash":"0442e600"},{"kind":"spec","id":"spec/05-units-and-providers.md#git","hash":"401cf59b"}],"by":"Doug ClymerOlson <doug@yarina.org>","note":"Reviewed against the section for the function it exercises: slug/1. The section says slug/1 'names the repository', and the test asserts the https and ssh spellings of one repository produce the same slug — which is what 'names the repository' means rather than 'names the URL'. The sentence #154 reworded is the rationale for the cheap key and changes no behaviour.","basis":"review"}
{"id":"3968261cfadb329c1445e51b8eaf25b70079562c01c43409f45887889b35f910","at":"2026-10-06T19:49:50Z","commit":"07848d66536b1a3bad4d89be4a23736a2193e195","parents":["b7e4b3c3c7e45cfa636880a965b8a490d2f5dd5ebf590e32fa252c89c5e21152"],"op":"relate","type":"implements","ends":[{"kind":"code","id":"Depdep.Provider.Git.slug/1","hash":"19529c5b"},{"kind":"spec","id":"spec/05-units-and-providers.md#git","hash":"401cf59b"}],"by":"Doug ClymerOlson <doug@yarina.org>","note":"Reviewed against the section for the function it exercises: slug/1. The section says slug/1 'names the repository', and the test asserts the https and ssh spellings of one repository produce the same slug — which is what 'names the repository' means rather than 'names the URL'. The sentence #154 reworded is the rationale for the cheap key and changes no behaviour.","basis":"review"}
{"id":"def36af2f3ba5d585b1167e7835c01a870c70431c643a99076d32ba381ba11f6","at":"2026-10-06T19:50:07Z","commit":"07848d66536b1a3bad4d89be4a23736a2193e195","parents":[],"op":"relate","type":"verifies","ends":[{"kind":"test","id":"Depdep.Provider.GitTest: collect then restore: a restored mirror is a sound repository, not just some files","hash":"ab0efa9d"},{"kind":"spec","id":"spec/05-units-and-providers.md#git","hash":"401cf59b"}],"by":"Doug ClymerOlson <doug@yarina.org>","note":null,"basis":"proposed"}
{"id":"695c1dbb1f16c60ff770ca78dc0bed038ce784ed40b23854ca0a65694e6f0be4","at":"2026-10-06T19:51:14Z","commit":"07848d66536b1a3bad4d89be4a23736a2193e195","parents":["def36af2f3ba5d585b1167e7835c01a870c70431c643a99076d32ba381ba11f6"],"op":"relate","type":"verifies","ends":[{"kind":"test","id":"Depdep.Provider.GitTest: collect then restore: a restored mirror is a sound repository, not just some files","hash":"ab0efa9d"},{"kind":"spec","id":"spec/05-units-and-providers.md#git","hash":"401cf59b"}],"by":"Doug ClymerOlson <doug@yarina.org>","note":"Reviewed against the section for the function it exercises: mirror_path/1. The section says mirror_path/1 is 'where a mirror lands', and the test restores an object and asserts a HEAD exists at that path and that git fsck accepts the result — so the path is where a mirror lands AND what lands there is a repository, not a directory of files. That second half is also the section's 'a mirror is only a seed' claim: a seed has to be a usable repository for the consumer's own fetch to reconcile. The sentence #154 reworded changes no behaviour.","basis":"review"}
{"id":"5d04b0befb6f4d2bfb4123e303c06221bbac8e95cdec8d2b2cbb37c712ad3d51","at":"2026-10-06T19:51:14Z","commit":"07848d66536b1a3bad4d89be4a23736a2193e195","parents":["64b518170765cdf658f4926d17bee2ea416168956328c8a2b50bd51dae26462f"],"op":"relate","type":"implements","ends":[{"kind":"code","id":"Depdep.Provider.Git.mirror_path/1","hash":"f7777182"},{"kind":"spec","id":"spec/05-units-and-providers.md#git","hash":"401cf59b"}],"by":"Doug ClymerOlson <doug@yarina.org>","note":"Reviewed against the section for the function it exercises: mirror_path/1. The section says mirror_path/1 is 'where a mirror lands', and the test restores an object and asserts a HEAD exists at that path and that git fsck accepts the result — so the path is where a mirror lands AND what lands there is a repository, not a directory of files. That second half is also the section's 'a mirror is only a seed' claim: a seed has to be a usable repository for the consumer's own fetch to reconcile. The sentence #154 reworded changes no behaviour.","basis":"review"}
{"id":"3d41ca83af9139f933b10cc2f5489d858bb43d90701daead976e131494485809","at":"2026-10-06T19:51:55Z","commit":"07848d66536b1a3bad4d89be4a23736a2193e195","parents":["7b742c71194c802525387a7c03fa9e9b3470d2fc7ca210c9d6bbbbad3c94c974"],"op":"retire","type":"implements","ends":[{"kind":"code","id":"Depdep.Lock","hash":"ce5497f8"},{"kind":"spec","id":"spec/00-prior-art.md#ci-caches","hash":"79566821"}],"by":"Doug ClymerOlson <doug@yarina.org>","note":"Not true, and recorded by my own bulk . #ci-caches is a prior-art comparison — what depdep is mistaken for — and Depdep.Lock implements no part of it. The section now MENTIONS Lock, because the measured instance it records is the lockfile key-normalisation defect Lock fixes (#153), and surfex reasonably suggested a relation from that citation. A mention is not an implementation. Lock's own relations are elsewhere and unaffected."}
